---
import SquarespaceFooter from '../../components/SquarespaceFooter.astro';
import SquarespaceHeader from '../../components/SquarespaceHeader.astro';
import type { ShellConfig } from '../../config/shell';
import { shellConfig } from '../../config/shell';
import SquarespaceShellLayout from '../../layouts/SquarespaceShellLayout.astro';

const { config = shellConfig }: { config?: ShellConfig } = Astro.props;

const IMG = '/lessons/punctuation2';

const navItems = [
  { href: '#prerequisites', label: 'Prerequisites' },
  { href: '#complete-complete', label: '"Complete | Complete"' },
  { href: '#run-ons', label: 'Run-ons' },
  { href: '#colons', label: 'Colons' },
  { href: '#commas', label: 'Commas' },
  { href: '#dashes', label: 'Dashes' },
  { href: '#strategies', label: 'Strategies' },
  { href: '#title-occupation-name', label: 'Title/Occupation + Name' },
  { href: '#niche-question-types', label: 'Niche question types' },
];
---
<SquarespaceShellLayout config={config}>
  <div id="siteWrapper" class="clearfix site-wrapper">
    <SquarespaceHeader config={config} />

    <main id="page" class="ht-main" tabindex="-1">

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 1 — HERO
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-hero">
        <div class="ht-pn-hero__inner">
          <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--crimson">SAT Writing &amp; Language</p>
          <h1 class="ht-pn-hero__h1">Punctuation (Boundaries)</h1>
          <p class="ht-pn-hero__lead">
            How to handle all Standard English Convention questions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           LEFT NAV + CONTENT LAYOUT
           ====================================================== -->
      <div class="ht-pn-layout">
        <aside class="ht-pn-nav" aria-label="Lesson sections">
          <nav class="ht-pn-nav__inner">
            <p class="ht-pn-nav__label">On this page</p>
            <ul class="ht-pn-nav__list">
              {navItems.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={item.href} class="ht-pn-nav__link" data-nav-link>{item.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>

        <div class="ht-pn-content">

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 2 — PREREQUISITES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="prerequisites">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Prerequisites</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Prerequisites</h2>
          </div>

          <h3 class="ht-pn-h3">Complete Sentences</h3>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            What are they, or rather, how should we handle them on the SATs?
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Exactly one Independent clause + literally any number of
            dependent clauses, including zero dependent clauses.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Many different types of questions hinge upon whether you can
            identify a complete sentence vs. an incomplete sentence: colons,
            semicolons, periods, comma-FANBOYS, run-on sentences.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>What is an independent clause?</strong> Has subject and
            verb. You can usually just tell. If it sounds like it could stand
            by itself, or if it sounds like a complete thought, it's probably
            an independent clause, and therefore a complete sentence in and
            of itself.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Examples</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>The committee reached a compromise after weeks of unproductive debate.</li>
            <li>The engineer revised the design so that it could withstand greater pressure.</li>
            <li>The student who had submitted her essay late asked for additional feedback.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>What are dependent clauses?</strong> Dependent clauses
            are phrases that cannot stand by themselves. There are some very
            common types that, if you recognize them, can help out greatly.
            You don't need to know the technical names, just learn the
            patterns.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Phrases that set time or place (prepositional phrases).</strong>
            Any phrase that starts with "As", "While", "In", "At", "Near",
            "Above", "Below", etc.
          </p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>In the 1950s</li>
            <li>While the board members debated the merits of the proposal</li>
            <li>Over twenty years ago</li>
            <li>Above the building</li>
            <li>Through the complex patterns in her classical music compositions</li>
            <li>Formed in 1967 to foster political and economic stability within the Asia-Pacific region</li>
            <li>When the manuscript was returned to the author with extensive notes and corrections.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Conditional phrases.</strong> Phrases that start with
            words like "Because", "If", "Although", "Since", etc.
          </p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>Since the creation of the oversight committee</li>
            <li>If the leverage ratio reaches a certain point</li>
            <li>Because these organisms are so tiny</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Phrases that start with "ing" words.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>Starting with the lowest number</li>
            <li>Solidifying her position within the organization</li>
            <li>Forming a layer of sedentary bedrock.</li>
            <li>Ensuring that proteins retain their original configurations</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Interjections.</strong> What are they and why are they so
            important? They are essentially dependent clauses that are placed
            within another clause. They are phrases that, if removed from
            the sentence, the sentence still flows. Usually, you'll find
            them within independent clauses, but on questions that are the
            most difficult, you'll see them within a dependent clause. They
            are explicitly tested with commas and dashes but are also used
            to make other types of question more difficult. Recognizing them
            and removing them from sentences can make questions far easier.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Examples</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>The company, though battered by years of declining profits, announced an ambitious new initiative.</li>
            <li>In <em>The Book of Salt</em>, for example, the exiled character of Bình connects to his native Saigon through the food he prepares</li>
            <li>The artwork, though controversial at its unveiling, has since become widely celebrated.</li>
            <li>The invention, which transformed communication across continents, changed history.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's some common patterns of sentence structure you'll see on
            the SAT.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Independent clause only:</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>The engineer revised the design so that it could withstand greater pressure.</li>
            <li>Beatrix Potter is perhaps best known for writing and illustrating children's books such as <em>The Tale of Peter Rabbit</em> (1902).</li>
            <li>The orchestra performed a piece that blended classical structure with modern dissonance.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Dependent, Independent.</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>In 1933, the Twentieth Amendment to the US Constitution was ratified.</li>
            <li>In her poetry collection <em>Thomas and Beulah</em>, Rita Dove interweaves the titular characters' personal stories with broader historical narratives.</li>
            <li>In a study of the cognitive abilities of white-faced capuchin monkeys (<em>Cebus imitator</em>), researchers neglected to control for the physical difficulty of the tasks they used to evaluate the monkeys.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Independent, Dependent.</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>The intense pressure found in the deep ocean can affect the structure of proteins in fish's cells, distorting the proteins' shape.</li>
            <li>The cognitive abilities of monkeys given problems requiring little dexterity were judged by the same criteria as were those of monkeys given physically demanding problems, such as unscrewing a bottle and inserting a straw.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="ht-pn-body"><strong>Dependent, Independent, Dependent.</strong></p>
          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>In the 1950s, a man named Joseph McVicker was struggling to keep his business afloat when his sister-in-law Kay Zufall advised him to repurpose the company's product, a nontoxic, clay-like substance for removing soot from wallpaper, as a modeling putty for kids.</li>
            <li>Compiled in the late 1500s largely through the efforts of Indigenous scribes, <em>Cantares Mexicanos</em> is the most important collection of poetry in Classical Nahuatl, the principal language of the Aztec Empire.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 3 — "COMPLETE | COMPLETE"
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="complete-complete">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">The core rule</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">"Complete|Complete"</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            These three types of punctuation marks require complete
            sentences before them AND after them: periods, semicolons, and
            comma+FANBOYS ("<span class="ht-pn-mono">,and</span>"
            "<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>"). Not just a comma, and
            not just a FANBOYS, but a comma right before a FANBOYS together
            forms one punctuation mark, equal to a period and semicolon.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            So, whenever you're testing one of these three, read up to the
            point and check if it's a complete sentence. If it's not, then
            it's incorrect. Then read from that point to the end of the
            sentence and do the same check. Let's work through some
            examples.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P1.png`} alt="Diagram: 'complete?' on both sides of a sentence boundary, arrows meeting at a dashed divider." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P2.png`} alt="Same diagram with boxes below showing a semicolon, a period, and a comma-plus-'and' as the possible boundary marks." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P3.png`} alt="Worked example: 'I took the SATs, and I crushed it.' with both halves labeled complete and checked." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's test the period in answer choice A. We read up to the
            period to see if it's complete, which it is. Read from after the
            period to the end of the sentence to see if that's complete,
            which it is. Therefore A works, it's the correct answer.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P4.png`} alt="SAT-style question about deep-sea fish proteins and TMAO, with both halves of answer choice A marked complete on either side of the period." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P6.png`} alt="Reasoning notes: 'Let's test A) — read up to the period and check if it's complete — read from after to the end and check. Since they both are, a period works.'" loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Example 2, 2, 19. Here we see a comma+FANBOYS
            ("<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>") in answer choice D. Read
            from the beginning of the sentence up to the
            "<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>". We see right away that
            it's not a complete sentence so D is out, since a comma+FANBOYS
            requires a complete sentence before and after.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P7.png`} alt="SAT-style question about video game graphics, testing a comma-plus-FANBOYS ('but') answer choice." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P10.png`} alt="Answer choice D crossed out, with a note showing the clause before the comma-plus-FANBOYS is incomplete." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Example 3, 1, 21. Here's an example when a comma+FANBOYS is
            correct, answer choice A.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P11.png`} alt="SAT-style question about marginalia in a book once owned by Walt Whitman, with comma-plus-FANBOYS answer choice A correct." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Example 3, 2, 26. Here's an example where we see that three
            answer choices contain one of the three "complete|complete"
            punctuations. We can see that no other words are changed among
            those three answers, meaning, logically, none of them can be
            correct. Indeed, if we were to test the phrase after, starting
            with "the stitching…", we'd see that it does not create a
            complete sentence.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P12.png`} alt="SAT-style question with three answer choices sharing a period, semicolon, or comma-plus-FANBOYS — all eliminated because the text after the boundary isn't complete." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 4 — RUN-ONS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="run-ons">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">What to avoid</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Run-ons</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>What they are.</strong> Run-ons are where you have two
            complete sentences connected by only a comma, only a FANBOYS, or
            by nothing.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P13.png`} alt="Diagram labeled 'Run-Ons' showing two complete sentences joined incorrectly by only a comma, only a FANBOYS word, or nothing, with a red X." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            On the SAT, they're testing almost always as incorrect answers
            when you see that there are answers with periods, semicolons,
            comma+FANBOYS, or colons (which we'll talk about soon).
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's revisit this problem. 2, 1, 22. We already saw that there
            were two complete sentences. One up to the word
            "configurations." and another from "TMAO" to the period. B
            separates them with nothing, C with only a comma, and D with
            only a FANBOYS ("and").
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P14.png`} alt="SAT-style question about deep-sea fish proteins and TMAO, revisited to test answers B, C, and D for run-ons." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P15.png`} alt="Diagram marking both halves of the TMAO sentence as complete, testing options between them." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P16.png`} alt="Annotation showing B (nothing), C (only a comma), and D (only 'and') are all run-ons." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            We see two completes, up to "value" and from "when"
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P18.png`} alt="SAT-style question with two complete sentences, one ending at 'value' and another starting at 'when.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P19.png`} alt="Answer choices tested for run-ons, with the correct comma-plus-FANBOYS choice identified." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Again, two completes, up to "percents" and from "such"
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P21.png`} alt="SAT-style question about plastic-bag taxes and the 'rebound effect,' testing the boundary between 'percent' and 'such.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P24.png`} alt="Diagram marking both halves of the sentence complete around the words 'percent' and 'such.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P22.png`} alt="Answer choices A, B, and D crossed out as run-ons, leaving the correct choice." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 5 — COLONS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="colons">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">A different pattern</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Colons</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>The rule for the SATs.</strong> Colons can be used in
            many different ways, but on the SATs, it goes like this: you
            MUST have a complete up to the colon, but what comes after just
            has to be an example or explanation of what came before it. It
            can be complete or incomplete. So notice it can have two
            completes but doesn't have to. Just make sure they're two
            different statements before and after.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's test answer choice A. From "The number" to "varied" is
            complete, so we're good so far. Then what follows "some
            shoppers" to the end of the sentence is an explanation of the
            first phrase. A it is.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P25.png`} alt="SAT-style question about a jam-tasting experiment, testing whether a colon can follow the complete clause 'The number of jams available for tasting varied.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P26.png`} alt="Annotation confirming the complete clause before the colon and the example/explanation relationship after it." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Running the same test, we see that B is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P28.png`} alt="SAT-style question running the same colon test, with answer choice B correct." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 6 — COMMAS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="commas">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">The two comma rules</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Commas</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Commas are mostly tested in two different ways: to bookend
            interjections and to separate independent and dependent clauses.
            Interjections have to be surrounded by two commas, or as we'll
            see, with dashes. However, it's much more common to see this
            done with commas.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            The other test of commas follows this rule: on the SATs we
            ALWAYS separate independent and dependent clauses.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Checkout this question. You notice that A) has "<span class="ht-pn-mono">,and</span>"
            but does not have a complete before it. That's out. Then let's
            check the answer with a comma. I read the sentence without the
            phrase now located inside two commas "a fictionalized … sisters,"
            and see that the sentence flows: <em>Julia Alvarez's 1994 novel
            In the Time of the Butterflies can serve as a starting point
            for…</em> It flows. Choice C is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P29.png`} alt="SAT-style question about Julia Alvarez's novel In the Time of the Butterflies, testing whether a phrase is a removable interjection." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P49.png`} alt="Answer choice A crossed out because the clause before 'and' is not complete." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P50.png`} alt="Confirmation that choice C is correct because the sentence still flows once the interjection is removed." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Another quick example. B) Since we can remove the phrase "for
            instance" choice B is correct. Notice none of the others have
            complete sentences up to the word "newts", which they all
            require, so we can eliminate them for that reason.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P45.png`} alt="SAT-style question testing whether 'for instance' can be removed as an interjection." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P51.png`} alt="Diagram showing the phrase 'for instance' isolated and tested for removability." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P52.png`} alt="Confirmation that removing the interjection leaves a complete, flowing sentence." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's two more examples that work the exact same way.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P53.png`} alt="SAT-style question about the 12th Amendment and US presidential elections, testing an interjection." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P54.png`} alt="SAT-style question about carbon-13 hibernation research, testing an interjection." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's check out an example of the second way the SAT tests comma
            usage: separating Independent and Dependent clauses. Notice that
            with C) and D) we do not have a required complete starting from
            "positing", and in D) we do not have an example or explanation
            (though it is close). Notice that phrase starts with an "ing"
            word, "positing"? A common type of dependent clause. And
            "Epicurus…soul" works as an Independent. Choice A it is.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P40.png`} alt="SAT-style question about Epicurus and pleasure, testing where a comma should separate an independent clause from a dependent clause." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P55.png`} alt="Diagram marking the independent and dependent clauses on either side of 'positing.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P56.png`} alt="Answer choices A and C circled with reasoning about which options avoid a run-on." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 7 — DASHES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="dashes">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Rarer, but predictable</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Dashes</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Dashes are tested the exact same ways as the two comma rules we
            just went over, though they are far less common on the SAT. A
            sentence can have only 0, 1, or 2 in a sentence. If you have
            one, it MUST be to separate Independent and Dependent clause. If
            you have two, they MUST be to bookend interjections. They rarely
            ask about Ind. and Dep clauses.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's an example. Notice there's already a dash in the
            sentence? A pretty big indicator we should test choice B. Read
            the sentence without the phrase "assuming solar panel
            installers' cooperation" and we see it flows. Choice B it is.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P44.png`} alt="SAT-style question about solar panel installers and pollinator populations, with one dash already present in the sentence." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P57.png`} alt="Confirmation that the interjection between dashes can be removed and the sentence still flows." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Another example that works the same way.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P32.png`} alt="SAT-style question about Sophie Calle's photography exhibition, testing dash placement." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P68.png`} alt="Diagram testing whether the phrase between dashes can be removed from the sentence." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P69.png`} alt="Confirmation that the phrase flows once removed, making the two-dash answer correct." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 8 — STRATEGIES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="strategies">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Strategies</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Strategies: common answer choice patterns and punctuation hierarchy</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 8A — TITLE/OCCUPATION + NAME
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="title-occupation-name">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Looks like a comma question — isn't</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Title/Occupation + Name</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            There are their own type of question. Though it looks like it's
            testing comma usage, you should handle it differently. The name
            of the question-type tells you exactly what it is: when you see
            a title/occupation right before a name. "SAT tutor Majid Hasan",
            "theoretical physicist Robert J. Oppenheimer", "cartoon
            character Krusty the Clown", etc.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            The vast majority of the time, no commas before or after the
            name.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Check out two examples.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P58.png`} alt="SAT-style question about screen actor Anna May Wong, testing whether commas belong around the title-plus-name construction." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P59.png`} alt="A second SAT-style example testing the title/occupation-plus-name pattern with no commas around the name." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>The exception:</strong> every once in a while, you'll
            need a comma after the name (never before) if there's an
            interjection right after the name (or maybe a dependant clause).
            "The book was written by legendary journalist Bob Woodward, who
            won a Pulitzer prize the following year." Here we'd need a comma
            after the name to separate the independent and dependent
            clauses.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's an example of an interjection after the name (it's from
            the ACT, but it's exactly what you might see on the SAT). See
            the interjection that starts right after "Ien Chi"? So no comma
            before the name, but we need one right after.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P60.png`} alt="ACT-style question showing an interjection right after the name 'Ien Chi,' requiring a comma after the name but not before it." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 8B — NICHE QUESTION TYPES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="niche-question-types">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Rare, but worth knowing</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Niche (infrequent) Question Types</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Multiple semicolons.</strong> If you see the option to
            have 2 semicolons in a sentence, meaning there's one in the
            sentence already and the answers would let you add another, it
            has to be a semicolon answer choice. Sometimes that's enough to
            answer the question, but if you have to choose, you need to
            follow the pattern in the sentence. See, the semicolons are
            being used like commas in a list.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            In this first example, it has be to B) since that's the only
            option that would have two semicolons in the sentence.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P61.png`} alt="SAT-style question where only one answer choice would create two semicolons in the sentence." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P63.png`} alt="Annotation confirming answer choice B as the only option producing two semicolons." loading="lazy" />
            <img src={`${IMG}/P62.png`} alt="Full question text showing the semicolon used as a separator in a list." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            In this second example, we have two options that would result in
            two semicolons. Therefore, we need to follow the pattern:
            "…dedicated; …fostering; and educating…"
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P64.png`} alt="SAT-style question where two answer choices would both produce two semicolons, requiring the existing list pattern to be followed." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>Two correct comp/comp answers (transitions).</strong>
            These questions will have two answer choices that are
            grammatically correct. They'll correctly link two completes. The
            question is really about which answer choice presents the
            transition word correctly. Think of them as having three
            statements. S1, S2, and S3. The question is whether S1 and S2
            contrast, or whether S2 and S3 contrast. So far, it's always
            been S1 and S2.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Notice that answer choice C) would imply that the idea that her
            subsequent career was more than just solving equations with the
            idea that she helped usher in the digital age? Rather, it should
            be that the idea that she solved equations in the war contrasts
            with the idea that the rest of her career was about more than
            just equations. Choice A makes this clear.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P70.png`} alt="SAT-style question about a mathematician's wartime and postwar career, testing which transition correctly links two complete sentences." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            This example is the same: Choice A) indicates Okinaka sits on
            the review board, but that he doesn't make decisions on his own.
            Choice B) would imply that him not making decisions alone
            contrasts with the notion that all decisions are made
            collectively by a group of experts, which doesn't make sense.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img src={`${IMG}/P67.png`} alt="SAT-style question about a sociologist on a historic-sites review board, testing which transition correctly links two complete sentences." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 9 — CTA STRIP
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-cta-strip">
        <h2 class="ht-cta-strip__h2">Want help putting this into practice?</h2>
        <p class="ht-cta-strip__body">
          These patterns click fastest with real SAT questions and someone
